2. If you, as a Dzongkhag Beautification Officer, were asked to host the next RBFE, how would you host it differently? (20 marks)', 'I would focus on lower cost and lasting benefit: plan early with a clear budget and seek sponsorship from businesses and partners (public-private partnership); use locally grown plants and materials, and involve local farmers, schools, monasteries and communities as growers months in advance; design the venue as a permanent park or public garden so the exhibition leaves a lasting legacy for the town; feature native and climate-suited plants and water-wise, zero-plastic, zero-waste practices; include workshops, competitions, a flower and plant market, buyer-seller meetings and value-chain exhibits so growers earn income; promote it digitally (virtual tours, social media) and link it with tourism packages; ensure accessibility and cultural programmes; and evaluate costs, visitors and economic impact afterwards.', null, '[{"point": "Cost-effective planning: budget control, use local materials/ plants, sponsorships/ PPP, reuse infrastructure", "marks": 5}, {"point": "Sustainability: permanent gardens/ parks as legacy, native plants, zero-waste, water-wise", "marks": 5}, {"point": "Inclusive and engaging: community/ schools/ farmers participation, competitions, workshops, market stalls, digital/ virtual tours", "marks": 5}, {"point": "Economic linkage: trade fair for growers, buyer-seller meets, tourism packages; monitoring and evaluation", "marks": 5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation'),
